<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Saxe-Eisenach</h1><p class="st-meta">1672 CE – 1741 CE · capital Eisenach</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ernestine Saxon duchy around the Wartburg, birthplace of Johann Sebastian Bach, later merged with Weimar.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the 1662 division of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a> was briefly held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-william-duke-of-saxe-eisenach">Adolf William</a>, whose line ended in 1671. In 1672 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-george-i-duke-of-saxe-eisenach">John George I</a>, previously lord of Marksuhl, received Eisenach as a separate Duchy of Saxe-Eisenach, founding the line that ruled it until 1741.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The duchy took in parts of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> in 1690. It was Lutheran, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg">Wartburg</a> castle, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Luther</a> had translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>, as its landmark. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a> in 1685, the son of a town musician, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-philipp-telemann">Georg Philipp Telemann</a> served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapellmeister">Kapellmeister</a> at the Eisenach court. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-wilhelm-duke-of-saxe-eisenach">Johann Wilhelm</a> ruled from 1698 to 1729.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-henry-duke-of-saxe-eisenach">William Henry</a> died without heirs in 1741, and the duchy passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-augustus-i-duke-of-saxe-weimar-eisenach">Ernest Augustus I of Saxe-Weimar</a>, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-weimar-eisenach">Duchy of Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach</a>, which lasted until 1918.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-saxe-eisenach.jpg" alt="Duchy of Saxe-Eisenach" width="320" height="280"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_Duchy_of_Saxe-Eisenach_(1680).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1672 CE – 1741 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Eisenach</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John George I, Johann Wilhelm, William Henry</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sachsen-Eisenach</dd></dl></aside></div>
